<BaseLayout title="How to Protect Your Privacy When Using AI Tools | TechPilot AI" description="A practical privacy checklist for AI tools: minimize sensitive data, review settings, understand retention, and use safer workflows.">
  <article class="prose article">
    <a class="text-link" href="/how-to/">← All How-To Guides</a>
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Digital safety · Practical guide</span>
      <h1>How to Protect Your Privacy When Using AI</h1>
      <p class="article-lede">AI tools can be useful for drafting, summarizing, and organizing work. Before you paste information into one, pause to consider what the service receives and how that information may be handled.</p>
      <p class="article-meta">A practical guide · Updated October 16, 2026 · 6 minute read</p>
    </header>
    <div class="notice"><strong>Safest default:</strong> Share the minimum information needed to complete the task. Never assume a tool is approved for confidential or personal data without checking its terms and your organization's rules.</div>
    <h2>1. Classify the information before sharing it</h2>
    <p>Separate public information from internal, confidential, personal, and highly sensitive data. Examples that deserve extra care include patient or customer records, identity documents, passwords, access tokens, financial details, private correspondence, and unpublished business plans.</p>
    <p>If you are unsure whether a category is allowed, do not upload it until you have checked the service terms and any workplace policy that applies.</p>
    <h2>2. Minimize and anonymize inputs</h2>
    <p>Ask whether the AI can complete the task with less information. Replace real names with labels such as “Customer A,” remove contact details, and use synthetic examples where practical. Check the entire prompt and attachments—not just the main text—for identifiers.</p>
    <h2>3. Review the provider's privacy information</h2>
    <p>Before using a service for anything beyond public or low-risk information, look for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Whether prompts and uploaded files may be stored, and for how long.</li>
      <li>Whether conversations may be used to improve models and what controls are available.</li>
      <li>Who can access the data, including service providers and authorized administrators.</li>
      <li>How to delete conversations, files, or an account.</li>
      <li>Whether business or education plans have different terms from individual accounts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Features and policies can differ by plan, region, and account settings. Check the current official policy instead of assuming that one product's settings apply to another.</p>
    <h2>4. Check settings and account security</h2>
    <p>Review data-use controls, chat history, connected apps, file access, and sharing settings where available. Use a unique password and multi-factor authentication if offered. Keep the account and browser up to date, and sign out on shared devices.</p>
    <h2>5. Treat connected tools as additional access</h2>
    <p>When an AI assistant connects to email, cloud storage, calendars, or other applications, review the requested permissions. Grant only the access required, disconnect integrations you no longer use, and be careful with instructions or files that ask an AI system to reveal private data or perform unexpected actions.</p>
    <h2>6. Review outputs before sharing them</h2>
    <p>An AI-generated answer may repeat sensitive details from your input or introduce unsupported claims. Check the result for names, account numbers, private content, and confidential material before sending, publishing, or saving it somewhere others can access.</p>
    <h2>7. Use workplace-approved tools for work data</h2>
    <p>For employer, customer, or patient information, follow the relevant privacy, security, and records-management policies. Ask the responsible administrator if you do not know which tools are approved. Do not move protected information into a personal account just because it is convenient.</p>
    <h2>A quick privacy checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Do I need to share this information at all?</li>
      <li>Can I remove names, identifiers, or unnecessary details?</li>
      <li>Have I checked the tool's current data-use and retention settings?</li>
      <li>Are the tool and account approved for this type of information?</li>
      <li>Have I reviewed connected-app permissions and sharing controls?</li>
      <li>Will I inspect the output before sharing it with others?</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>If you accidentally share sensitive information</h2>
    <p>Follow your organization's incident-reporting process if work or regulated data may be involved. Use the service's available controls to remove the content where possible, but do not assume deletion from the visible chat removes every copy or record. If credentials were exposed, rotate them promptly and notify the appropriate administrator.</p>
    <p>For better results without oversharing, see our guide to <a href="/how-to/write-better-ai-prompts/">writing clearer AI prompts</a>. To check the information you receive, read <a href="/how-to/verify-ai-answers/">how to verify AI answers</a>.</p>
